csv - emit loop or archive data in comma-separated-value format
Copyright 2015-2021 Matthew Wall
Distributed under terms of the GPLv3

This service will emit either loop or archive data from weewx in CSV format.
Typically this would be done using a template and the Cheetah reporting engine
in weewx.  But this service provides the option of emitting loop data, not just
archive data.

===============================================================================
Installation

The independent 0.12.0 continuation targets WeeWX 5.5.2–5.6 and Python
3.10–3.14, with no extra Python dependencies.

Install the published archive with WeeWX 5's extension installer:

```sh
weectl extension install https://github.com/weewx-packages/weewx-csv/releases/download/v0.12.0/weewx-csv-0.12.0.zip
```

Use the `weectl` from your WeeWX environment. Add
`--config /path/to/weewx.conf` when the station configuration is not found
automatically. Package installations may require `sudo`; activate the WeeWX
virtual environment for pip installations. Restart your WeeWX service after
configuration, for example `sudo systemctl restart weewx` on systemd.
The installer does not restart it for you.

The installer registers user.csv.CSV in process_services and installs
bin/user/csv.py. Configure a filename in a directory writable by WeeWX:

[CSV]
    filename = /var/tmp/weather.csv
    binding = archive
    mode = overwrite
    append_datestamp = False
    fields = outTemp, outHumidity

After restart this example replaces one snapshot per archive record.
Use mode = append for a log. There is no separate report or HTTP server.
The service writes the packet's units without converting them.
Back up custom configuration before upgrading with a newer archive.
To remove it, run weectl extension uninstall csv and restart WeeWX.


===============================================================================
Configuration

The service recognizes a few options, including:

binding - The binding determines whether the file will be updated with every LOOP packet or archive record. Possible values are loop or archive. Default is loop

filename - The full path of the file. Default is /var/tmp/data.csv

mode - Indicates whether to append or overwrite each time data is written to file. Default is append.

append_datestamp - Indicates whether the file should include a timestamp of the form YYYY-mm, so that /var/tmp/data.csv becomes /var/tmp/data-2017-01.csv, /var/tmp/data-2017-02.csv, etc. Default is True.

datestamp_format - The format for the appended datestamp. For example, %Y-%m-%d would result in filenames such as data-2017-10-01.csv, with a new file each day. Default is %Y-%m, which results in a new file each month.

header - Indicates whether to include a header with the name of each field at the beginning of the file. Default is True.

timestamp_format - The format for the data timestamp. For example, %Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S results in a dateTime field like 2017-10-01 14:23:03. All times are UTC. Default is no format, which results in a unix epoch.


===============================================================================
Examples

[CSV]
    filename = /tmp/weewx-data.csv
    header = False


Independent continuation

This repository continues https://github.com/matthewwall/weewx-csv from
34a9a52e5fedb47af216c281084f03a833fd75e6. It is maintained separately.
The full upstream history and GPLv3 license are retained.

Changes in 0.12.0

Overwrite mode publishes a complete header and packet using an atomic rename
in the output directory. A failed write leaves the previous snapshot intact.
Append mode retains its existing behavior. CSV strings are now quoted when
needed, so commas and quotes do not create additional columns.

Loop packets can contain different observations on each event. Configure fields
for fixed columns, or use binding = archive for consolidated archive records.
Absent configured fields are empty. Default columns remain packet-dependent.

fields - Optional ordered list of observation names, with dateTime always first.

decimal_places - Optional number of fractional digits for floating-point values,
from 0 to 15. Integers and dateTime are unchanged. Default preserves str(value).

Example:

[CSV]
    mode = overwrite
    append_datestamp = False
    fields = outTemp, outHumidity, batteryStatus
    decimal_places = 2
